          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I was told my thyroid is underactive.",
          "answer": "Your clinician's finding that your thyroid is underactive is the medical term hypothyroidism. The educational reference \"Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)\" explains that the thyroid gland makes too little thyroid hormone. That hormone helps set how fast most of the body's tissues work. The pituitary gland, a small gland in the brain, responds to low levels by releasing more thyroid-stimulating hormone. That is why this hormone is usually the first test. The reference says the great majority of cases start in the thyroid itself.\n\nThe search did not return the rest of that document. So I can't tell you its symptoms, causes, or treatment options from the sources I have.\n\nAsk your clinician:\n- What test results led to this finding, and what do the numbers mean for me?\n- Do I need further tests, and how often will my levels be checked?\n- What symptoms should prompt me to call back sooner?\n\nIf you develop severe symptoms, such as chest pain, severe breathlessness, fainting, or confusion, seek urgent medical care straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
